  "executive_briefing": "As of mid-2026, global corporate tax regimes and international trade policies are defined by broad structural alignment on the OECD Pillar Two minimum tax, escalating bilateral trade friction, and the rapid deployment of digital transaction controls. Over 140 jurisdictions continue enforcing baseline effective tax rates under Pillar Two, backed by updated OECD reporting frameworks and administrative guidance. Meanwhile, international commerce experiences acute volatility as new executive tariffs, such as those impacting Canada and the broader U.S. trade landscape, provoke retaliatory measures and complex litigation. Regionally, ASEAN economies like Singapore balance competitive statutory frameworks with tailored tax rebates, even as they roll out rigid e-invoicing timelines like the mandatory InvoiceNow mandates scaling through 2031.",
